**Theorem 5.1 (uniqueness of contact masses and grouped matrices).**
For every sufficiently small positive $p$, the optimal tuple in EP.14
at $\tau(p)$ is uniquely

$$
\begin{aligned}
X_{+,+}&=\pi_{++}h_{++}h_{++}^T+\pi_{+-}h_{+-}h_{+-}^T,\\
X_{-,+}&=\pi_{-+}h_{-+}h_{-+}^T,\qquad
X_{-,-}=\pi_{--}h_{--}h_{--}^T,\qquad X_{+,-}=0.
\end{aligned}
\tag{EP.30}
$$

It has the ranks EP.8. The slacks $S_{+,+},S_{-,+},S_{-,-}$ have
ranks $1,2,2$, respectively, and $S_{+,-}$ can have rank three.

Proof. Equality in EP.29 saturates contrast and all PSD pairings.
Null-decompose any optimal slots by Proposition 2.2. Every nonzero
atom must lie on the appropriate null zero ray listed after EP.27.
The first four moment columns are a perturbation of the invertible
corner Hadamard matrix, so the four total masses are unique. They are
precisely $\pi_j(p)$. All are positive; the two positive-$u$ rays are
independent. This proves both grouped uniqueness and the primal ranks.
It permits proportional splitting of an atom and makes no claim of
unique encoder or Kraus representation.

The first PSD slack kills two independent contacts, and is nonzero
because its null-cone zero set is not the whole ellipse. Its rank is
one. Each other used slack has exactly one null-cone zero. Near that
contact its ellipse quadratic form equals $(2/p)(P-H_p)$ and has
strictly positive second derivative. If its PSD rank were one, its
form would be a square of a linear form. The affine zero line on
$h_0=1$ either meets the ellipse transversely, giving a second
intersection, or is tangent, making the square have zero second
derivative at the contact. Both contradict the established properties.
It cannot be zero or PD, so its rank is two. Strict completion gives
the unused block rank three. The rank statements concern complete
slack matrices, not only their restrictions to the null cone.
$\square$

